        fp: "Defendant wanted to rob an occupied single-family residential home at night. At 2:00 AM, Defendant approached the home wearing a dark ski mask. Defendant walked to a ground-floor bedroom window and found the window closed, but completely unlocked. Defendant placed his hands under the wooden sash, lifted the window open eighteen inches, and stepped across the sill with both feet into the darkened bedroom. While standing on the bedroom carpet, Defendant was confronted by the homeowner, who had been woken by the window opening and was pointing a loaded 12-gauge shotgun at Defendant's chest. The homeowner shouted, 'Freeze or you're dead!' Defendant immediately raised his hands, fell to his knees, and begged the homeowner not to shoot. The homeowner detained Defendant until police arrived five minutes later. Defendant had not touched any property in the home. The jurisdiction adheres to traditional common law criminal definitions.",
        q: "What is the most serious crime of which Defendant can properly be convicted?",
        opts: [
            "Common law burglary.",
            "Attempted burglary only.",
            "Attempted larceny only.",
            "Criminal trespass to real property only."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "Rule: At common law, burglary requires: (1) breaking, (2) entering, (3) the dwelling house, (4) of another, (5) in the nighttime, (6) with the intent to commit a felony therein. Under common law breaking rules, opening an unlatched, closed door or lifting an unlatched, closed window constitutes an actual physical breaking because the actor applies physical force to move a structural closure to create an opening. Stepping across the sill with both feet constitutes completed entry. The structure was an occupied dwelling house, the act occurred at 2:00 AM (nighttime), and Defendant entered with the intent to steal property (larceny, a common law felony). Crucially, the crime of burglary is fully complete at the precise instant of unlawful entry with felonious intent; whether the intended felony is completed or interrupted by an armed resident is completely irrelevant. Defendant committed completed common law burglary (Option A). Options B, C, and D are incorrect because completed burglary is an aggravated felony that ranks above all attempts or trespasses."
