        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under well-established proximate cause principles (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 447 and Restatement (Third) of Torts § 34), an intervening force does not sever proximate causation if the intervening force was foreseeable, or if the resulting harm falls squarely within the scope of the risk created by the defendant's initial negligence. When a commercial truck negligently causes a highway collision that disables a passenger vehicle in the roadway or on a shoulder, the precise foreseeable risk created by that initial accident is that subsequent highway travelers will fail to observe the obstruction in the dark or rain and collide with the stopped vehicles, injuring motorists standing at the scene. Subsequent collisions by inattentive drivers at an active highway accident scene are deemed foreseeable intervening forces, not superseding causes; the freight carrier remains concurrently liable for the aggravated injuries (Option C). Option A is incorrect because inattentive drivers rear-ending disabled vehicles at highway crash scenes are foreseeable, not extraordinary superseding causes. Option B is incorrect because inspecting one's car on a shoulder after a crash is not voluntary assumption of the risk. Option D is incorrect because trucking companies sound in negligence, not strict liability."
