        topic: "Mixed",
        fp: "A master commercial stone mason entered into a written contract with an art museum to build an ornate exterior granite fountain in the museum's sculpture garden for $60,000, payable in three milestone installments of $20,000 as construction progressed. The contract specified that the stone carving would be completed according to detailed architectural blueprints. The mason completed the excavation and rough concrete basin (milestone one) and received the first $20,000 payment. Before the mason could begin dressing and setting the decorative granite blocks, an accidental electrical fire originating in an adjoining commercial property spread across the alley, destroying the museum's sculpture garden and causing a large brick structural wall to collapse across the fountain site. The municipal building department condemned the area and issued a permanent demolition order forbidding any construction on the site for three years. The museum refused to pay the mason any further money. The mason had incurred $14,000 in direct labor and specialized stone-cutting expenses for the second milestone prior to the fire, but had not yet set the stones in place.",
