        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under the modern doctrine of impossibility/impracticability and frustration of purpose (Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 261, 265), a supervening government order or physical destruction of the site that permanently prohibits construction discharges both parties from further executory contractual duties. However, under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 377, discharge for impossibility or frustration does NOT leave a performing party without a remedy: a party whose duty of performance is discharged after rendering partial performance or incurring substantial reliance expenses that confer value or prepare customized materials for the project is entitled to restitution (quantum meruit / reliance reimbursement) for the reasonable value of the performance rendered prior to the discharging event (Option B). Option A is incorrect because expectation damages (the full contract price) are barred when performance is excused by supervening impossibility. Option C is incorrect because modern contract law rejects the archaic English rule (Chandler v. Webster) that left losses where they fell; restitution is granted to prevent unjust forfeiture. Option D is incorrect because lost profits cannot be recovered when performance is discharged without breach."
