        fp: "A plaintiff visited a commercial archery range to practice target shooting. The range consisted of ten parallel shooting lanes separated by six-foot-high plywood partition dividers. The range owner had built the partition dividers using ordinary half-inch interior-grade plywood. In the adjacent lane, a novice archer using a high-draw-weight compound hunting bow experienced a mechanical release malfunction, causing an arrow with a steel target point to discharge sideways at an acute angle. The steel-tipped arrow struck the plywood partition divider, penetrated completely through the half-inch wood, and struck the plaintiff in the shoulder, causing deep muscle lacerations. Architectural safety standards published by the National Archery Association specify that indoor lane dividers must be constructed of ballistic Kevlar mesh or two-inch solid tongue-and-groove hardwood to prevent arrow pass-through. The plaintiff sued the range owner for negligence. The range owner moved for summary judgment, arguing that the plaintiff had signed a written exculpatory waiver before shooting that released the range from 'all claims, liability, and injuries arising from the inherent risks of archery target shooting.'",
