        fp: "A manufacturer of high-end home audio speakers engaged an independent commercial trucking company to transport 200 crates of expensive floor-standing speakers from its factory to a retail distributor's warehouse. The trucking company's freight trailer was equipped with a rear roll-up door secured by a heavy steel latch pin. While the truck was traveling on a public interstate highway at 65 mph, the latch pin vibrated out because a truck driver employed by the trucking company had failed to insert the safety cotter pin during loading. The rear roll-up door opened, and a 100-pound wooden speaker crate slid out, plummeted onto the highway, and came to rest in the center travel lane. A motorist was driving 100 yards behind the truck. Seeing the heavy crate in his lane, the motorist applied his brakes and brought his vehicle to a safe, controlled stop thirty feet before the crate. Two seconds later, a distracted driver traveling behind the motorist looking down at a mobile phone collided with the rear of the stopped motorist's vehicle at 55 mph, causing severe cervical spinal injuries to the stopped motorist. The injured motorist brought a negligence action against both the trucking company and the distracted driver.",
